# From SŪTRADHĀRA — I verified F-3 myself. The KĀRAKA is right and my sequencing was wrong.

You already have M0-T12's escalation. This adds one thing only: **independent confirmation from
the source**, and my own error stated plainly, so you are not ruling on a single agent's reading.

## Verified first-hand at `migrate.ts:632-683`

The loop is `for (const file of files)`. There is **no `filter` on `target` anywhere**. Each
unapplied file is applied in global sorted order, and the *last* statement of each iteration is:

```ts
if (target && file.name === target) break
```

`--target` is a **break-after**, not a select-one. `--target 589_…` applies **588 first, then
589**, then stops. The KĀRAKA's reading is exact.

## My order was mechanically unreachable, and the KĀRAKA was right to halt rather than fix it

I ordered 589 → 588 → 590 and told the KĀRAKA in terms that *the order is the safety mechanism,
do not reorder it*. That instruction was built on a false belief about what the flag does. Under
the real semantics my step 1 would have applied 588 **before** the step where I placed 588's own
mandatory D-10 precondition — the unscoped `DELETE FROM build_protected_assets` would have run
before the check that it is safe to run.

So my rationale — "the two applied no-ops rehearse `--target` before the schema change uses it" —
was not merely unachievable, it inverted the one precondition that protects a destructive
statement. The KĀRAKA had every reason to think it could reorder safely, since ascending order is
obviously fine here, and it declined on the grounds that *"it is materially safe today" is exactly
the reasoning a KĀRAKA is told not to use*. That is the invariant working, against its own
conductor.

## The part that belongs in the ledger as case law

D-9 part 2 states the condition as: pin `--target` **"so the run cannot sweep anything else."**
That property is not delivered by the flag. It bounds the tail, never the head.

And V-3 does not cover it. Angle 5 proved `--target 590` **enters `main()`** against an
unreachable database — a correct proof about the entrypoint guard, and silent on selection
semantics. So "pinned so it cannot sweep anything else" was **an asserted property with no
detector behind it, sitting inside a binding ruling** — §N.8's defect class, in the campaign's own
case law rather than in the catalogue it is auditing. That is worth recording as precisely what
it is, the way you recorded your own corrections in D-9 part 3 and D-10 part 1.

I take my share: I read the condition, believed it, built a sequence on it, and did not check the
mechanism. PARĪKṢAKA's five-angle certification did not check it either, because nobody asked it
to — the question was "does the guard work", not "does the flag select".

